# .spool/invariant.req

feature: INVARIANT

requirements:

  - id: INVARIANT-1
    title: Invariants Tracked Like ACs
    description: >
      A requirement may list invariants alongside its acceptance
      criteria. Spool parses, indexes and links them to annotated
      tests the way it does ACs, and reports an untested invariant
      as a finding with its own configurable severity, error by
      default.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-05
    rationale: >
      An invariant is a property that must hold across every run of
      a requirement, successful or not, so it needs a test as much
      as any AC does. Until now Spool ignored the invariants list,
      so a missing invariant test went unnoticed: in the first agent
      run (SLICE-7) nothing would have caught one. The ID format
      <REQ>-<n>-INV-<n>, for example SLICE-6-INV-1, was chosen
      over <REQ>-<n>-I<n> because it stands out when scanning a file.
      Like untested_ac, untested_invariant defaults to error, because
      tracing every item to a test is the point of the tool.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Editor support for invariants (LSP diagnostics, hover, navigation,
        code lens and the traceability view)
      - Checking that an AC or invariant ID belongs to the requirement
        it's listed under
      - Per-item status on invariants or ACs
      - Command-line flags and output formats (`spool check` stays text)
      - Migrating existing specs and markers to the new format
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: INVARIANT-1-1
        title: Invariant ID Format
        description: >
          Given an ID of the form <REQ>-<n>-INV-<n>,
          when it is validated and normalized,
          then it is valid, and normalizing it upper-cases the
          prefix and INV and strips leading zeros from the numbers
          ("slice-06-inv-01" becomes "SLICE-6-INV-1"), while
          "SLICE-6-INV", "SLICE-INV-1", "SLICE-6-I1" and
          "SLICE-6-INV-1-1" are malformed.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-2
        title: Invariants Parsed
        description: >
          Given a requirement with an invariants list whose items
          have an id, a title and a description,
          when the .req file is parsed,
          then each invariant is read with its normalized ID and its
          1-based line number, alongside the requirement's ACs, and
          a malformed invariant ID produces a parse warning naming
          the ID, as a malformed AC ID does.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-3
        title: Invariant Annotations
        description: >
          Given a test function annotated with an invariant's ID in
          any supported comment style, with or without text after a
          colon,
          when the tests are scanned and the index is built,
          then the invariant is linked to that test function, and an
          annotation naming an invariant ID that no .req file
          defines is reported as orphaned.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-4
        title: Duplicate Invariant ID
        description: >
          Given two invariants with the same ID, in the same .req
          file or different ones,
          when the index is built,
          then an index error names the duplicated ID and both
          source files, as a duplicate AC ID does.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-5
        title: Untested Invariants Reported
        description: >
          Given an active requirement with an invariant that no test
          annotates,
          when the text report is generated,
          then it lists the invariant's ID and description under its
          own "Untested Invariants" heading, counts invariants,
          linked invariants and untested invariants per feature and
          in the totals, separately from ACs, and says "Full
          traceability" only when there are no untested ACs, no
          untested invariants and no orphaned annotations.
          Invariants of requirements that aren't active are neither
          counted nor listed.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-6
        title: Untested Invariant Severity
        description: >
          Given severity.untested_invariant in .spool.yaml,
          when config is loaded and spool check runs on a project
          with an untested invariant,
          then it defaults to error when not set, error makes spool
          check exit 1, warning, info and off leave the exit code to
          the other findings, and a value other than error, warning,
          info or off is a config error naming the value, as for the
          other severities.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-7
        title: JSON and Markdown Reports
        description: >
          Given a report with invariants,
          when it is formatted as JSON or Markdown,
          then the output carries the same invariant counts per
          feature and in the totals, and the list of untested
          invariants with each one's ID, description and feature,
          and every existing JSON field keeps its name and value.

      - id: INVARIANT-1-8
        title: Invariants Documented
        description: >
          Given spool init writes SPOOL.md,
          when it is read,
          then it describes the invariants list in a .req file, the
          <REQ>-<n>-INV-<n> ID format with an example, and how to
          annotate a test with an invariant's ID.

  - id: INVARIANT-2
    title: Editor Support for Invariants
    description: >
      The LSP server gives invariants the editor support it gives
      ACs: navigation both ways between an invariant and its tests,
      document links, diagnostics, code lenses, hover, and a place
      in the traceability tree.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-06
    rationale: >
      INVARIANT-1 made Spool track invariants but left the editor out,
      so in VS Code an invariant ID is inert text: no jump to its
      tests, no warning when it has none, and no entry in the
      traceability view. Moving between a spec and the tests that
      cover it is the main reason to use the LSP, and an invariant
      needs that as much as an AC. Requirement IDs summarize their
      ACs today, so they summarize their invariants too.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - The VS Code extension's tree view; it follows in spool-vscode
      - Applying the configured severities to diagnostics, which ACs
        don't do either
      - New LSP capabilities or custom requests
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: INVARIANT-2-1
        title: Go to Definition for Invariants
        description: >
          Given a test annotated with a known invariant's ID, and that
          invariant's id line in a .req file,
          when go-to-definition is invoked on the annotation line,
          then the LSP returns the invariant's line in the .req file;
          and when it is invoked on the invariant's id line, it
          returns the locations of all linked test functions, or an
          empty result when the invariant has no tests.

      - id: INVARIANT-2-2
        title: Document Links for Invariants
        description: >
          Given a .req file with invariants and a test file annotated
          with a known invariant's ID,
          when the documents are opened,
          then a tested invariant's ID is a link to a covering test,
          an untested invariant's ID is not a link, and the
          annotation is a link to the invariant's definition.

      - id: INVARIANT-2-3
        title: Diagnostics for Invariants
        description: >
          Given an active requirement with an invariant that has no
          covering tests, and another that has,
          when the index is built,
          then a warning diagnostic naming the untested invariant's
          title and ID is published on its id line, none is
          published for the tested one, and an annotation naming an
          invariant ID that no .req file defines gets the orphaned
          annotation warning.

      - id: INVARIANT-2-4
        title: Code Lenses for Invariants
        description: >
          Given a .req file with invariants and a test file annotated
          with a known invariant's ID,
          when code lenses are requested,
          then a tested invariant shows its test count with a command
          to navigate to the tests, an untested one shows "untested",
          and the annotation shows the invariant's title with a
          command to navigate to its definition.

      - id: INVARIANT-2-5
        title: Hover for Invariants
        description: >
          Given a test annotated with a known invariant's ID, and that
          invariant's id line in a .req file,
          when hover is invoked on the annotation,
          then the tooltip shows the invariant's title, description
          and parent requirement; and when it is invoked on the
          invariant's id line, the tooltip shows the linked test
          function names and files, or "No covering tests".

      - id: INVARIANT-2-6
        title: Requirement Summaries Include Invariants
        description: >
          Given a requirement whose invariants have tests,
          when hover, go-to-definition or document links are used on
          the requirement's id line,
          then the hover lists each invariant with its test count or
          "untested" after the ACs, go-to-definition returns the
          invariants' tests after the ACs' tests, and the document
          link, which already targets the first AC test, targets an
          invariant's first test when no AC has one.

      - id: INVARIANT-2-7
        title: Invariants in the Traceability Tree
        description: >
          Given a requirement with invariants,
          when the spool/traceability request is handled,
          then the requirement carries an "invariants" list in
          definition order, each entry with its id, title, file, line
          and testCount as an AC entry has, the list is empty rather
          than missing when there are none, and the "acs" list is
          unchanged.
